Sure, here's an example of unit testing, integration testing, and acceptance testing in Java code:
1. Unit Testing:
Unit testing is the process of testing individual units or components of a software application in isolation. In Java, JUnit is a popular framework used for unit testing. Here's an example of a unit test for a simple Java method that adds two numbers:
```java
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;
public class CalculatorTest {

  @Test
  public void testAdd() {
    Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
    int result = calculator.add(2, 3);
    assertEquals(5, result);
  }
}
public class Calculator {
  public int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
  }
}
```
In this example, we're testing the `add` method of the `Calculator` class. We create an instance of the `Calculator` class and call the `add` method with two numbers. We then use the `assertEquals` method from the JUnit framework to verify that the result of the `add` method is equal to the expected value.
2. Integration Testing:
Integration testing is the process of testing how different components of a software application work together. In Java, we can use frameworks like TestNG or JUnit to perform integration testing. Here's an example of an integration test for a Java web application:
```java
import org.junit.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.junit4.SpringRunner;
import org.junit.runner.RunWith;
@RunWith(SpringRunner.class)
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
public class WebAppIntegrationTest {
  @Autowired
  private TestRestTemplate restTemplate;
  @Test
  public void testGetUsers() {
    ResponseEntity<List<User>> responseEntity =
        restTemplate.exchange("/users", HttpMethod.GET, null, new ParameterizedTypeReference<List<User>>() {});
    List<User> users = responseEntity.getBody();
    assertEquals(2, users.size());
  }
}
public class UserController {
  @GetMapping("/users")
  public List<User> getUsers() {
    return userService.getUsers();
  }
}
```
In this example, we're testing the `getUsers` method of a `UserController` class in a Java web application. We use the `TestRestTemplate` class from the Spring framework to send an HTTP GET request to the `/users` endpoint of the web application. We then use the `assertEquals` method to verify that the response contains two users.
3. Acceptance Testing:
Acceptance testing is the process of testing a software application to ensure that it meets the requirements and specifications provided by the customer or end user. In Java, we can use frameworks like Cucumber or FitNesse to perform acceptance testing. Here's an example of an acceptance test for a Java web application:
```java
import io.cucumber.java.en.Given;
import io.cucumber.java.en.Then;
import io.cucumber.java.en.When;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;
public class WebAppAcceptanceTest {
  private WebDriver driver;
  @Given("I am on the login page")
  public void i_am_on_the_login_page() {
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "/path/to/chromedriver");
    driver = new ChromeDriver();
    driver.get("http://localhost:8080/login");
  }
  @When("I enter my credentials")
  public void i_enter_my_credentials() {
    WebElement usernameInput = driver.findElement(By.id("username"));
    WebElement passwordInput = driver.findElement(By.id("password"));
    WebElement loginButton = driver.findElement(By.id("login-button"));
    usernameInput.sendKeys("myusername");
    passwordInput.sendKeys("mypassword");
    loginButton.click();
  }
  @Then("I should be logged in")
  public void i_should_be_logged_in() {
    String pageTitle = driver.getTitle();
    assertEquals("Dashboard", pageTitle);
    driver.quit();
  }
}
public class LoginController {
  @PostMapping("/login")
  public String login(@RequestParam String username, @RequestParam String password) {
    if (username.equals("myusername") && password.equals("mypassword")) {
      return "dashboard";
    } else {
      return "login";
    }
  }
}
```
In this example, we're testing the login functionality of a Java web application using Cucumber. We use the ChromeDriver to simulate a user opening the login page, entering their username and password, and clicking the login button. We then use the `assertEquals` method to verify that the user is redirected to the dashboard page after successfully logging in.

