The double dabble algorithm is a method used to convert binary numbers into decimal numbers. It involves shifting and adding digits in a binary number until it is in a format that can be easily converted to decimal.
Here's the step-by-step process for the double dabble algorithm:
1. Create an array of bits to represent the binary number.
2. Initialize a counter variable to keep track of the number of times the bits have been shifted.
3. While the counter is less than the length of the array, perform the following steps:
   a. Shift all the bits in the array to the left by one position.
   b. If the most significant bit (MSB) is 1, add 3 to the second-to-last bit.
   c. If the third-to-last bit is greater than or equal to 5, add 3 to the second-to-last bit.
   d. If the second-to-last bit is greater than or equal to 8, add 1 to the last bit.
   e. Increment the counter.
4. Convert the resulting number to decimal.
5. Round the decimal to the nearest integer.
Here's an implementation of the double dabble algorithm in C programming language:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
int main() {
    int binary[] = {1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1}; // example binary array
    int length = sizeof(binary) / sizeof(binary[0]);
    int counter = 0;
    while (counter < length) {
        for (int i = 0; i < length - 1; i++) {
            binary[i] = binary[i] << 1;
            if (binary[i + 1] >= 5) {
                binary[i] += 3;
            }
        }
        if (binary[length - 1] >= 8) {
            binary[length - 1] -= 8;
            binary[length - 2] += 1;
        }
        counter++;
    }
    int decimal = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        decimal += binary[i] * pow(2, length - i - 1);
    }
    decimal = round(decimal);
    printf("Binary: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        printf("%d", binary[i]);
    }
    printf("\nDecimal: %d", decimal);
    return 0;
}
```
This implementation takes an example binary array of {1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1}, performs the double dabble algorithm, and outputs the resulting binary and decimal numbers. Note that the `round()` function is used to round the decimal number to the nearest integer.

