Here's an example code snippet in C# that splits a string into an array of words, shuffles them randomly, and then joins them back into new sentences:
```
using System;
using System.Linq;
class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        string input = "This is a string containing multiple sentences. Each sentence should be shuffled randomly.";
        string[] sentences = input.Split(new char[] { '.', '!' }, StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries);
        Random rng = new Random();
        foreach (string sentence in sentences) {
            string[] words = sentence.Split(new char[] { ' ', ',', ';', ':' }, StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries);
            words = words.OrderBy(x => rng.Next()).ToArray();
            string shuffledSentence = string.Join(" ", words);
            Console.WriteLine(shuffledSentence);
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we first split the input string into an array of sentences using the `Split` method, specifying the period and exclamation mark as delimiters. We then loop through each sentence and split it into an array of words using the `Split` method again, this time specifying several common punctuation marks as delimiters.
Next, we use LINQ's `OrderBy` method to shuffle the words randomly, using a `Random` object to generate a random number for each word. Finally, we join the shuffled words back into a new sentence using the `Join` method, with a space as the separator.
Note that this example assumes that each sentence ends with a period or exclamation mark, and that there are no other instances of these characters within the sentences. If your input string contains more complex sentence structures, you may need to modify the code to handle them correctly.

