Sure, here's the PHP code for converting a quadratic equation in the form of ax^2 + bx + c to both standard and expanded form:
```
function quadraticEquation($a, $b, $c) {
  // Calculate the discriminant
  $discriminant = pow($b, 2) - 4 * $a * $c;

  // Calculate the roots of the equation
  $root1 = (-$b + sqrt($discriminant)) / (2 * $a);
  $root2 = (-$b - sqrt($discriminant)) / (2 * $a);

  // Calculate the coefficients of the standard form
  $coefficients = "{$a}x^2";
  if ($b > 0) {
    $coefficients .= "+{$b}x";
  } elseif ($b < 0) {
    $coefficients .= "{$b}x";
  }
  if ($c > 0) {
    $coefficients .= "+{$c}";
  } elseif ($c < 0) {
    $coefficients .= "{$c}";
  }

  // Calculate the equation in expanded form
  $expanded = "{$a}x^2";
  if ($b > 0) {
    $expanded .= "+{$b}x";
  } elseif ($b < 0) {
    $expanded .= "{$b}x";
  }
  if ($c > 0) {
    $expanded .= "+{$c}";
  } elseif ($c < 0) {
    $expanded .= "{$c}";
  }
  $expanded .= "=(x-{$root1})(x-{$root2})";

  // Output the results
  echo "Standard form: {$coefficients}<br>";
  echo "Expanded form: {$expanded}";
}
// Test the function
quadraticEquation(1, -5, 6);
```
