What are the differences between using HPLC, SFC, and GC for chromatographic separation? Can you provide a comparison of the advantages and limitations of each technique?
Here's an example C# code for implementing HPLC:
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
namespace HPLC
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            //initialize HPLC parameters
            double flowRate = 0.2; //mL/min
            double columnLength = 50; //mm
            double particleSize = 5; //μm
            double columnInnerDiameter = 4.6; //mm
            //calculate HPLC retention time
            double retentionTime = (columnLength * particleSize) / (flowRate * columnInnerDiameter);
            Console.WriteLine("The HPLC retention time is: " + retentionTime + " minutes.");
            Console.ReadLine();
        }
    }
}
Can you modify this code to implement SFC or GC instead? And how do the resulting chromatograms compare in terms of separation efficiency and resolution?

output:
Sure, here's an example C# code for implementing SFC:
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
namespace SFC
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            //initialize SFC parameters
            double flowRate = 2; //mL/min
            double columnLength = 100; //mm
            double particleSize = 2; //μm
            double columnInnerDiameter = 4.6; //mm
            //calculate SFC retention time
            double retentionTime = (columnLength * particleSize) / (flowRate * columnInnerDiameter);
            Console.WriteLine("The SFC retention time is: " + retentionTime + " minutes.");
            Console.ReadLine();
        }
    }
}
And here's an example C# code for implementing GC:
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
namespace GC
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            //initialize GC parameters
            double columnLength = 30; //m
            double filmThickness = 0.25; //μm
            double columnInnerDiameter = 0.25; //mm
            double flowRate = 1; //mL/min
            //calculate GC retention time
            double retentionTime = (columnLength * filmThickness) / (flowRate * columnInnerDiameter);
            Console.WriteLine("The GC retention time is: " + retentionTime + " minutes.");
            Console.ReadLine();
        }
    }
}
In terms of separation efficiency and resolution, HPLC is typically used for separating small molecules, while SFC is used for separating larger molecules such as chiral compounds and peptides. GC is used for separating volatile compounds. The efficiency and resolution of each technique depend on various factors such as the type of stationary phase, the temperature, and the flow rate. Generally, HPLC and SFC provide higher resolution and efficiency than GC.

